---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CreateTableGenerator from '../../components/CreateTableGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL CREATE TABLE Generator" description="Define columns in plain text and generate a CREATE TABLE statement instantly.">
  <CreateTableGenerator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>How to use this</h2>
  <p>List each column as <code>name: type, constraints</code> — supported constraint keywords are primary key, not null, unique, and default. The tool assembles a standard SQL CREATE TABLE statement.</p>

  <h2>Common CREATE TABLE Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting a primary key</strong> on a new table, which most applications and ORMs expect by convention.</li>
    <li><strong>Using inconsistent naming conventions</strong> (mixing camelCase and snake_case) across columns in the same table.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This CREATE TABLE Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly scaffolding a new table's schema during development</li>
    <li>Getting correct CREATE TABLE syntax without looking up exact keyword order</li>
    <li>Prototyping a schema design before finalizing it in a migration</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>CREATE TABLE FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this support foreign keys?</strong> Not in this generator — add foreign key constraints separately with the Foreign Key Generator, or an ALTER TABLE statement after creating the base table.</p>
  <p><strong>Can I set a column as auto-increment?</strong> Specify it as a constraint matching your database's own syntax (e.g. <code>serial</code> for PostgreSQL, <code>auto_increment</code> for MySQL), since this keyword varies by database engine.</p>
</ToolLayout>
